= Distributed system =

== Overview ==
A '''distributed system''' is a system in which multiple autonomous computational components communicate and coordinate over a network to provide a service or computation.

The components do not share one perfectly synchronized memory or clock.

== Characteristic problems ==
Distributed systems must deal with:

* communication latency;
* partial failure;
* concurrency;
* message loss, duplication, or reordering;
* independent clocks;
* replicated or partitioned state;
* changing membership.

These problems make distributed coordination different from ordinary single-process computation.

== Network and distributed system ==
A [[Computer network]] provides communication among devices.

A distributed system adds coordinated application behavior, shared protocols, state, failure handling, and system-level semantics.

Therefore:

<pre>
computer network ≠ distributed system
</pre>

== Replication ==
Replication stores or computes copies across nodes for availability, locality, throughput, or fault tolerance.

Copies then need rules for synchronization and conflict resolution.

== Consistency ==
Distributed systems use several notions of consistency describing what operations can observe and in what order.

These are not identical to [[Consistency (logic)|logical consistency]].

Examples include linearizability, serializability, causal consistency, and eventual consistency.

== Fault tolerance ==
A fault-tolerant system continues to provide specified behavior despite some component failures.

Assumptions about crash failures, Byzantine failures, network partitions, synchrony, and quorum structure determine what guarantees are possible.

== Coordination ==
Consensus protocols can let nodes agree on values or replicated state despite failures under stated assumptions.

Coordination costs latency and availability, especially under partitions or wide-area delay.

== Related concepts ==
[[System boundary]], [[Computer network]], [[Consistency (logic)]], and [[Distributed cognition]] distinguish computational distribution from network substrate, logical consistency, and cognitive-system distribution.

